LEAN SIX SIGMA -- NOTES

What it is
1. Lean Six Sigma joins two methods with one aim: the best quality, cost, and delivery for the customer.
2. Lean removes waste, meaning any step that adds no value for the customer, so work flows faster.
3. Six Sigma reduces variation in a process, so the process makes fewer defects and errors.
4. Lean relies on simple tools, such as kaizen, workplace organization, and visual controls.
5. Six Sigma relies on data tools, such as statistical analysis, design of experiments, and statistical process control.
6. Teams often start with Lean to clean up the workflow, then use Six Sigma to make that workflow consistent.

Lean -- the five principles (Womack and Jones, 1996)
1. Value -- define value from the end customer's point of view.
2. Value stream -- map every step that delivers that value, and cut the steps that add none.
3. Flow -- make the value-adding steps run in tight sequence, with no stops.
4. Pull -- make only what the next step or the customer asks for.
5. Perfection -- repeat the cycle until value is perfect and waste is zero.

Lean -- the three Ms (Toyota Production System)
1. Muda -- waste, meaning work that uses resources and adds no value.
2. Mura -- unevenness in the pace of work.
3. Muri -- overburden on people or equipment.

Lean -- the 8 wastes (first letters spell DOWNTIME)
1. Defects -- Efforts caused by rework, scrap & incorrect information
2. Overproduction -- Production that is more than needed or before it is needed
3. Waiting -- Wasted time waiting for the next step in the process
4. Non-utilized potential -- Not using people's talents, skills & knowledge to their full
5. Transport -- Unnecessary movements of products & materials
6. Inventory -- Excess products & material not being processed
7. Motion -- Unnecessary movements by people (eg. walking)
8. Extra processing -- Unnecessary processing or activities in the process that do not add value
Note: Taiichi Ohno of Toyota named the first seven wastes, and the Haldan Consulting image adds non-utilized potential as the eighth.

Six Sigma -- DMAIC, the five steps of a project
1. Define -- state the problem, the goal, and what the customer needs.
2. Measure -- collect data on how the process performs today.
3. Analyze -- find the root causes of defects and variation.
4. Improve -- test and put in place fixes for those root causes.
5. Control -- keep the gains with monitoring and standard work.

Six Sigma -- the target
1. A process at the six sigma level makes no more than 3.4 defects per million opportunities.

